.TH "RAIL0" "1" "Aug 2026" "rail0 CLI" ""

.SH NAME
rail0-auth-logout - End the session: revoke the token on the gateway and remove it locally


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBrail0 auth logout [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
End the current session.

.PP
The token is revoked on the gateway — its jti goes into the denylist, so the JWT
stops being accepted BEFORE it expires — and then removed from this machine.

.PP
Removing the local copy alone is not a logout: the JWT stays valid for the rest
of its lifetime, so anyone holding another copy keeps the session. The local file
is removed even when the revocation cannot be performed (an unreachable gateway
is a normal reason to log out), and the outcome says which of the two happened.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for logout


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--agent\fP[=false]
	Agent mode: one JSON envelope on stdout, JSON events on stderr, no prompts (env: RAIL0_AGENT=1)

.PP
\fB--base-url\fP=""
	RAIL0 gateway base URL (env: RAIL0_BASE_URL)

.PP
\fB--debug\fP[=false]
	Print HTTP request/response details to stderr

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	Force plain (uncolored) JSON output (useful in scripts)

.PP
\fB--pretty\fP[=false]
	Use the key-value/table view instead of JSON (terminal only)

.PP
\fB-q\fP, \fB--quiet\fP[=false]
	Print only the result's id/hash, one per line (useful in scripts)


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBrail0-auth(1)\fP
